          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Homotopy type theory
            is a flavor of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            – specifically of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            – which takes seriously the natural interpretation of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+types"} path types
            as formalizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space+objects"} path space objects
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
            which have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+universes"} univalent universes
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+observational+type+theory"} higher observational type theory
          %p
            In the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics+of+homotopy+type+theory"} categorical semantics of homotopy type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            are interpreted not as
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional type theory
            can serve as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-categories"} 1-categories
            (such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretoposes"} pretoposes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+categories"} locally cartesian closed categories
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+toposes"} elementary toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            (such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-categories
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} presentations
            of (∞,1)-categories using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type-theoretic+model+categories"} type-theoretic model categories
            and related structures such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+systems"} weak factorization systems
          %p In addition to a viewpoint on identity types and a general class of categorical models, homotopy type theory is characterized by new homotopically motivated axioms and type-theoretic structures. Notable among these are:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Voevodsky’s
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifiers"} object classifiers
                in (∞,1)-categorical semantics.
            %li
              %p
                Strong
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+type"}> Higher inductive types
                , which among other things enable the construction of finite
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimits"} (∞,1)-colimits
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cell+complexes"} cell complexes
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truncated"} truncations
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizations"} localizations
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
          %p
            With all of these axioms included, homotopy type theory behaves like the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language+of+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} internal language of an (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            — see below.
          %p
            Many details are still being worked out, but the impression is that homotopy type theory thus should serve as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation"} foundation
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            that is natively about
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            — in other words, a foundation in which
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#Advantages Advantages
          %p
            As a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation"} foundation
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+foundations+for+mathematics"} univalent foundations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                It treats
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
                natively. This is an advantage for doing homotopical and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher-categorical
                mathematics, which is spreading slowly into other fields.
            %li
              %p
                It inherits the good computational properties of intensional
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
            %li
              %p
                It is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
                by default, but can easily be made
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical
                by adding axioms. This makes it potentially more expressive at essentially no cost. (In fact, it is not entirely clear how possible it is to do homotopy theory constructively in other foundations.)
            %li
              %p
                It can (conjecturally) be
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalized
                in many categories and higher categories, providing an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
                which enables a single
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
                to be reinterpreted in many places with many different meanings.
            %li
              %p
                It is naturally
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                = succeed "-invariant" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                and makes no reference to homotopy theory) which is expressible in the theory must be invariant under isomorphism/equivalence.
            %li
              %p
                Notions such as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
                and sets are
                %em defined
                objects, which inherit good computational properties from the underlying type theory.
            %li
              %p
                It treats sets,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groupoid"} higher groupoids
                on an equal footing. One can easily remain entirely in the fragment of the theory which talks about sets, not worrying about groupoids or homotopy theory, but as soon as one starts to say something which naturally needs structures of higher
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+level"} homotopy level
                (such as talking about some collection of structured sets), the groupoidal and homotopical structure is already there.
          %h3#models_in_categories_and_toposes
            Models in
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            It is well known that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+categories"} locally cartesian closed categories
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %p
            The step from extensional to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            that this brings with it makes intensional dependent type theory have models in certain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system"} weak factorization system
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
          %p
            It is conjectured (due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Awodey09"} Awodey 09
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Awodey12"} Awodey, 2010
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Joyal11"} Joyal, 2011
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Awodey%27s+conjecture"} Awodey's conjecture
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                intensional dependent type theory with dependent sums and products and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
                (a form of homotopy type theory) is an internal language for
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-categories
            %li
              %p
                with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
                added, it becomes an internal language for
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
          %p Indeed:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presentable
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-category
              has a presentation by a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type-theoretic+model+category"} type-theoretic model category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              (without, possibly, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
          %p
            For more on this see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-category
            in the section on internal logic.
          %p
            With the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            axiom included (for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+types"} type of types
            “weakly a la Tarski”) then homotopy type theory has categorical semantics in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+types"} type of types
            interpreted as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifier"} object classifier
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/homotopytypetheory/show/model+of+type+theory+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} model of type theory in an (infinity,1)-topos
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory#HomotopyWithUnivalence"}
                  relation between type theory and category theory — univalent homotopy type theory and elementary
                  = succeed "-toposes" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            For a short introduction to a simplicial model of homotopy type theory see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Streicher14"} Streicher 2014
          %h3#new_axioms New Axioms
          %p
            As a foundation for mathematics whose basic objects are higher groupoids, homotopy type theory makes visible new foundational axioms. Most of these axioms are true as statements about classical
            = succeed "-groupoids," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead%27s+principle"}> Whitehead's principle
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets+cover"}> sets cover
                , and more generally
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-types+cover"} n-types cover
            %li
              %p
                Several variants of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %p
            Additionally, since homotopy type theory as the internal language of
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty, 1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kock-Lawvere+axiom"} Kock-Lawvere axiom
            may be added to ordinary topos theory.
          %ul
            %li
              Axiom R (
              %a{:href => "cohesive+homotopy+type+theory#Shulman15"}> Shulman 15
              )
          %h3#relation_to_representation_theory Relation to representation theory
          %div
